      "side": 1,
      "message": "TetheringRequest objects aren\u0027t unique, an app can construct two identical ones and equals() will return true. Is this a case where \"\u003d\u003d\" would be correct? If not, maybe at least add a comment explaining why it cannot happen that there are ever two requests in the list for which equals() will return true?\n\nIn this CL we still have the invariant that there can never be more than one request for a given type, so we can write that in this comment, but future CLs are going to change that, and we\u0027ll need to update this comment accordingly (or use \"\u003d\u003d\" instead of equals()).\n\n    final TetheringRequest r \u003d new TetheringRequest.Builder(TETHERING_BLUETOOTH).build();\n     mTm.startTethering(r, () -\u003e {});\n     mTm.startTethering(r, () -\u003e {});\n\nUsing \"\u003d\u003d\" would avoid this problem, though \u003d\u003d is usually risky in other ways. Another option would be: every time we create a PendingRequest (or every time Tethering accepts a TetheringRequest from an app), it could assign the PendingRequest or the TetheringRequest an auto-incrementing ID, like we do for NetworkRequest.",
